% Bibliography.
%
% The organizer's 2026 specification prescribes the reference formats:
%   book    [n] author, title, place: publisher, page range, year.
%   journal [n] author, title, journal, volume(issue): page range, year.
%   web     [n] author, resource title, URL, access date (y-m-d).
% Conference papers are entered in the journal pattern, with the proceedings
% as the venue, because the specification defines no separate conference form.
%
% A book entry must carry a page range: the specification requires a book
% citation to state its pages. Keep the `pages` field populated for every
% book entry, or the rendered reference list silently drops it.
%
% Every entry below was checked against a primary bibliographic record before
% inclusion: the proceedings or publisher page, Crossref, or the arXiv API. A
% work with a formal version is cited in that version, with that version's
% metadata only; a work cited from arXiv carries its version number and that
% version's year. No entry mixes the metadata of two versions. A reference
% whose full page range could not be verified was omitted rather than
% completed from memory. The style does not print DOIs or URLs, so none is
% recorded. Only entries cited in the manuscript are kept; the list is ordered
% by first citation by the bibliography style.
%
% Note: BibTeX has no comment syntax. It scans for an entry wherever it sees
% an at-sign, including inside a line that starts with a percent sign, so an
% at-sign must never appear in these notes.

